# Backward compatibility: none until `8.0.0`, then ADR 329

## Until `8.0.0`

Every release is a release candidate and may break. Change the implementation directly. Do NOT add:
- Backward compatibility code, aliases or deprecation warnings
- Comments explaining "legacy" or "old" vs "new" approaches
- Tests, fixtures or examples that keep an old pattern beside the new one

Remove the old code, update every reference, tests and design docs to the current state, and record upgrade instructions for users (`record-upgrade-instructions` skill).

## From `8.0.0`

[ADR 329](../../docs/architecture%20docs/adrs/ADR%20329%20-%20An%20application%20built%20on%20Prisma%208%20upgrades%20within%20the%20major%20without%20changes.md) applies. An application or extension built on one `8.x` release works on every later one, so:
- Nothing Generally Available is removed or changed incompatibly within a major. A replaced API stays, deprecated, until the next major.
- A later release reads every stored file and database record an earlier release wrote.
- Internal code (not exported by a published package) and anything below GA ([ADR 330](../../docs/architecture%20docs/adrs/ADR%20330%20-%20A%20maturity%20level%20is%20written%20in%20a%20package's%20version%20and%20an%20API's%20name.md)) still changes directly, as above.
